ying the 8. What if an opponent plays a small card? If Right plays a 9, Left plays a J? I can wait to see what they do. I can play the cards later.\n\nHere\'s my full plan: Play ♠8. The lowest card in my hand, clear it and have a chance to clear more\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I have 5 single cards remaining in my hand (8, 10, K, 2, Black Joker). My goal is to clear these singles. I possess strong control cards (King, 2, and Black Joker) which gives me a good chance to regain the lead after playing my lower cards. The standard and most effective strategy in this situation is to play "low to high", clearing the small cards while using the high cards to beat opponents\' responses and maintain initiative. Leading with my highest cards (Black Joker or 2) would leave me with vulnerable low cards (8, 10) at the end, which is risky.\n\nReason: The ♠8 is the lowest card in my hand. By playing it first, I force the opponents to play higher cards to beat it. I have the King, 2, and Black Joker to cover most responses (except potentially the Red Joker or the last remaining 2 if held by opponents). This strategy maximizes the utility of my high cards to protect my low cards.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠8"]}'}
